<JONQC@CUNYVM.CUNY.EDU> wrote:

>> I'm trying to write a procedure to do a "bulk create" of accounts
>> (for setting up accounts for CSci classes). I've figured out how to
>> get the adduser portion to take its input from a script, but it appears
>> that regardless of what I do the passwd program always reads from the
>> terminal.
> 
>We had the same problem.  At the beginning of every semester, we need to
>put up bulk accounts for new students.  The answer to your problems is
>the 'expect' program that is mentioned in the OSF FAQ; 'expect' can
>"talk" to 'passwd'.
> 
>The quickest way to go is:  Copy the 'expect' program from the
>OSF/1 Freeware disc, modify the 'adduser' script to ask for the
>password (which you've probably done already), and then add a line to
>'adduser' to let 'expect' do the rest:
> 
>                setpass ${USER} ${PASSWD}
> 
>where the 'setpass' script contains the following:
> 
>#!/usr/local/bin/expect
>set password [lindex $argv 1]
>spawn passwd [lindex $argv 0]
>expect {*quit):}
>send p\r
>expect {*password:}
>send $password\r
>expect {*password:}
>send $password\r
>expect eof
> 
>NOTE: The FAQ file says that lines 2 & 3 should read "index" instead of
>"lindex".  However, the current copy of 'expect' that we downloaded from
>ftp.cme.nist.gov (you also need to download 'tcl' if you're going to
>make 'expect' from scratch) requires "lindex".  I don't know whether the
>version on the Freeware disc uses "index" or "lindex" but that's easy to
>find out.
> 
>FYI:  We usually set the initial password to the student's social
>security number.  If you're running C2 security (you probably should
>be), you can force them to change their initial password right away by
>pre-expiring it.  To do this, add the following 2 lines to 'adduser'-
> 
>#Delete 'u_succhg' entry in auth file to pre-expire the password
>ed -s "${AUTHDIR}/${USER}" < /usr/sbin/adduser.ed_commands
> 
>Where the file 'adduser.ed_commands' contains the following 3 lines-
> 
>1,$s/u_succhg#[0-9]*://
>w
>q
> 
>Admittedly, I'm still kind of a novice Unix user so maybe someone on
>the Net can figure out how to accomplish the pre-expiration all on one
>line that doesn't require a second input file but for now, this works.
> 
>Hope this helps.
