X-Deja-AN: 122948385
distribution: world
references: <4bknun$cja@news.internetmci.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: lueck@zk3.dec.com (Greg Lueck Development Environment)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.osf.misc


The ar command creates temporary files as it runs.  By default, these files
are created in the /tmp directory.  It appears that /tmp is on the root (/)
partition on your system and there is not enough room there for the temporary
files.  You can tell ar to create the temporary files in another directory
by setting the TMPDIR environment variable.  Set this variable to the name
of a directory in which ar should create temporary files.  For example, you
might set it like this:

	% setenv TMPDIR /usr/tmp

The /usr/tmp directory is mounted on the /usr partition which has much more
space on your system.  You may also be interested in the -l switch documented
on the ar(1) manpage.

