>        :ty=winchester:dt=SCSI:ns#84:nt#19:nc#2621:rm#5411:\
>
>Rotation speed is rm and all of capacity is used !

In this case, it is OK. But take the case of a disk that has more sectors on
its outer tracks than on its inner ones (NT# is not constant). These are not
uncommon. I have a Fujitsu that does this. There is no way I could configure
the disk in a disktab entry which would not waste space. And, since a SCSI dosk
is addressed by block number and not absolute location, there is no reason for
the OS to care.

And don't forget the non-zero possibility of a typo slipping through. That
could prove awkward. The drive always knows its exact configuration. Why not
simply ask it?

