In article <4b9r2k$4u7@solaris.cc.vt.edu>,
Randy Marchany <marchany@vtserf.cc.vt.edu> wrote:
>1) I have an ADVFS fileset spread across 3 volumes. Does ADVFS write
>   to volume 1 then volume 2 then volume 3 or does it spread the I/O
>   across the individual drives? In other words, does it do sequential
>   or parallel i/o when filling up a fileset?

It allocates *space* in a manner that tends to give the same % full to
each volume of the set.  This does not necessarily balance the i/o
load if you have hot spots.  One particular hot spot will be the ADVFS
log area.  Allocating the space to an LSM stripe set mounted as a
single volume to ADVFS should balance the i/o better, but I haven't
done that.

>3) My application (GIANT POP mail server) does a huge amount of writes
>   (small files < 2K in size). Is there anything I can do to optimize
>   ADVFS write?

I use a 3000/700 for the exact same application as you describe.
Since I configure sendmail to queue everything, my biggest hot spot is
the sendmail queue area.  Presto will help speed up individual
synchronous write performance, but if you're like me you'll still have
i/o rates that exceed the physical capacity of the drives.  I've just
ordered a Quantum SSD (solid state disk) which I'm going to use to
give myself an order of magnitude or so more ops/sec capacity for the
small-write dominated hot spots.

I'd like to compare notes with you if you'd be interested.  

