Fred Zwarts <ZWARTS@KVIASV.KVI.nl> wrote:

> [...] About 18 months ago, Digital told us that in about 2 years OSF/1
> would have similar cluster capabilities as VMS. We then decided to wait
> with the conversion. Now this two year period is coming to its end, I
> wonder how the situation is. What are the cluster capabilities of Digital
> Unix? I am not aware of much progess, did I miss something?

Yes and no.  The current Digital UNIX clusters ("AdvantageCluster"), are
good at high availability.  If a system goes down, a buddy system can
start up the application and continue running.  This works well for
relatively stateless apps (eg, file serving) and for apps that are
intelligent about abort behavior (esp, Oracle, Sybase, etc).

No Unix, from Digital or anyone else, really provides the large number
of nodes, the single system image for managment purposes, or the
coordinated cluster-wide locking (DLM) that VMS has had seemingly
forever.

Like a lot of things in moving from VMS to Unix, you'll have to decide
what you really need, and what you can live without.  Triage is
essential:  "Yeah, I like VMS' simple cluster-wide management, but I can
live without it if I can get the application availability."  OR  "No, I
really need a multi data center failover, and Unix can't do it."  If
you're waiting for Unix clusters to do absolutely everything VMSclusters
do, you'll wait at least another 2 years--and more likely, forever.

See also http://www.illuminata.com/vmsunix/chapter3.html/#397, which
includes a discussion of the differences between VMSclusters and Unix
clusters.


