If you are doing a port, the Digital Porting Assistant can help you.
It is available free.  It runs on Digital UNIX 3.2 and later.  At the
end of this message, I have attached a white paper that describes the
product.

Karen Michaels
Project Leader Digital Porting Assistant
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

                  Digital Porting Assistant
 Version 1.0 of the Porting Assistant is available now at no
   cost!  Version 2.0 is undergoing field testing through
                       January, 1996.

Benefits

The Porting Assistant is a tool that helps application
developers port their code to Digital UNIX(TM) from other UNIX
and proprietary platforms.  It identifies C, C++, and
Fortran code that may not compile or run properly on Digital
UNIX.  The Porting Assistant reduces the time and cost of
porting an application by automating many manual tasks,
including, quick access to technical porting information.

Features

The Porting Assistant's interactive environment lets
developers locate code that may need to be changed in order
to work on Digital UNIX.  The Porting Assistant also helps
developers understand why they need to make the changes and
then facilitates the changes.  For example, the Porting
Assistant provides a global replace capability to replace
text across a set of files.

Developers use the Porting Assistant to run different types
of checks, which in turn produce diagnostic messages
indicating potential problems in the code to be ported.
Developers can navigate though the diagnostic messages using
an editor (vi, Emacs or a supplied Motif editor) to view
both the diagnostic and the associated source code.

The Porting Assistant also makes it easier for developers to
link their code by helping to resolve undefined symbols.

Checking code...

The Porting Assistant checks find the following potential
porting problems:

- Conditional code (#ifdef) that might also need a
  Digital UNIX branch.

  Some applications that developers port to Digital UNIX
  may already run on multiple platforms.  If this is the
  case, the applications may use conditional code that
  applies to one or more platforms.  The Porting Assistant
  can locate this conditional code, which helps the
  developer decide if the code applies to Digital UNIX.

- Includes (#include) of files that don't exist; and
  locations where a file exists with the same name.

  Different operating systems store include files in
  different directories.  The Porting Assistant identifies
  include files that are not found in the specified
  location.  If possible, it reports locations where the
  file does exist.  For example, the file dkio.h is located
  in /usr/include/sun on SunOS systems, but it is located
  in /usr/include/sys on Digital UNIX systems.  The Porting
  Assistant will flag references to /usr/include/sun/dkio.h
  and suggest that the user include /usr/include/sys/dkio.h
  instead.

- Calls to library functions that do not match Digital
  UNIX definitions.

  The Porting Assistant checks for functions that do not
  have function definitions.  This may be the case if an
  application calls a system function that is available on
  another platform, but does not exist on Digital UNIX.
  The Porting Assistant provides mappings from many of
  these functions to equivalent Digital UNIX functions.
  The Porting Assistant also flags functions that have
  calls that are inconsistent with their definition.  This
  may occur if a function has a different signature on
  Digital UNIX than on another platform.

- Code with 32-bit dependencies.

  The Porting Assistant locates code that assumes it is
  running on a 32-bit architecture.  This includes
  operations such as assigning or casting pointers to
  integers, assignments of longs to other types, and sign
  extension problems.

- Calls to library functions with different semantics on
  Digital UNIX.

  The Porting Assistant provides a list of functions that
  have different semantics on Digital UNIX than on other
  platforms.  There are different categories of functions
  differences, including platform differences and 32/64 bit
  differences.  For each different function, the Porting
  Assistant provides access to the function's man page as
  well as a special man page that describes how this
  function is different on Digital UNIX.  The Porting
  Assistant searches the developer's code to find calls to
  these functions.

- Commands in the developer's makefile that may not run
  correctly on Digital UNIX.

  The Porting Assistant checks the actions in the
  developer's makefile to ensure that all the actions exist
  on the user's path.  If it cannot find an action, it may
  suggest locations of a program with the same name.
  The Porting Assistant checks the arguments and options to
  common build commands (cc, ld, ar, and rm).  It tells the
  developer when the Digital UNIX option is different from
  its source platform and what the equivalent Digital UNIX
  option is.

Linking the application...

The Porting Assistant helps resolve undefined symbols
reported by the linker.  It includes a facility to look up a
function to see what library defines it.  It reports the
library name and the linker options that are needed to link
the library with the application.

Accessing HyperHelp on porting...

The Porting Assistant includes online HyperHelp that
explains how to use the Porting Assistant and its checks.
The HyperHelp also includes porting tips that cover topics
such as byte ordering, data alignment, and shared libraries.
The extensive Help for the diagnostic messages is
particularly useful because it is directly linked to
specific messages with a description of the problem and
suggestions on how to fix it.

Replacing text in a set of application files...

As they port their application, developers may have to
change function names, variable names, or type names in
their source files.  The Porting Assistant lets developers
change a specified string to a different string in a set of
files.  The Porting Assistant performs the substitution with
or without confirmation.  If the developer wants to confirm
each substitution, the Porting Assistant brings each file
into an editor, positioned on each occurrence of the
specified string.  The developer can then choose to change
the current occurrence of the string.

Requirements

The Porting Assistant requires an Alpha machine with the
following configuration:
- 64 MB of memory
- Digital UNIX Operating System V3.2 or greater
- Digital UNIX Software Development Utilities V3.2 or greater
- 17 MB of disk space

How to get it

To schedule a demonstration or obtain a copy of the Digital
Porting Assistant, call or send mail to:
