x-mailer: Mozilla 1.1N (X11; I; OSF1 V3.2 alpha)

Here's a simple program for which the DEC OSF C++ compiler generates a (to me)
mysterious warning:

// foo.h:
class junk
        { double a;
          public:
          junk(void) {};
          junk(double x) { a = x;};
          junk & operator = (junk & x) { a = x.a; return(*this);};
        };

// foo.c
#include <iostream.h>
#include <math.h>
#include "foo.h"


main()
{
    junk x;

    x = junk(2);
}

The warning is

foo.c:10: warning: In this statement, the initialization of a non-const
reference requires a temporary for "junk(2)".

Programs which generate this warning nevertheless appear to execute correctly.
Is the DEC compiler getting excited about nothing?  Is it warning me about
nonportable usage?  Bad style?  What's the scoop?  If I'm doing something
wrong, is there a reasonably easy workaround?  Note that the line which reads
"x=junk(2)" can be replaced with "x=2" and still generates a similar warning.
Finally, I should mention that code like this generates no warnings with the
somewhat old Borland compiler on my home machine.

Thanks in advance for any help you can offer.
-- 
