abbot@ayrton.eideti.com (Nicholas Abbot) wrote:
>	I have a friend who bought an Alpha Internet
>	server.  You know the ones with all the
>	internet tools.  Anyway, he bought it without a
>	tape drive and later added one.  It's your
>	garden variety DEC dat SCSI tape drive.
>
>	Can anyone tell me how to get DEC Unix to "see"
>	the tape and a simple way to test it?  Imagine you
>	just installed the drive, what's next after
>	the hardware installation?  I've done this before
>	with SCO Unix, but I've long since killed that brain
>	cell.
>
>	Reply via mail, my news is slow.
>
>	Thanks, NIK (UNIX neophyte)


Nik,
  I am also a neophyte in UNIX.  But I just added a tape 
drive to a DEC Alpha this weekend.  Wasn't very hard.  Have 
your friend look in the System Administrators book, there 
should be a section on adding device to the operating 
system.  I am using OSF/1 on my DEC box.  I had to make a 
copoy of the current Unix kernal that is in root to a safe 
place (just in case), copy over the current kernal with a 
generic kernal (genvmunix), shutdown the server, turn 
off then on the Alpha, show devices (to find out what the 
Alpha sees on the SCSI bus, make a note of the addresses 
(0, 1, 2, etc.  this will include the tape drive if it is 
on the SCSI bus), restart the Alpha to single user mode, 
goto the /dev directory, look up in the sys admin book the 
type of drive and the device name, do a MAKEDEV with the 
device special names for "EACH" device that showed on the 
show device earlier, wait until the kernal has been 
rewritten to account for the devices, shutdown/halt the 
server again, change the user mode (single to multi mode 
for me), reboot the server.  that was it.  took me about 4 
hours to do this (I was looking in the sys admin book every 
2 minutes because I am paranoid about screwing up) install 
and the tape backups also.

Hope this helped,
scaldw@metc.doe.gov




