Subject: Re: BookReader threads example crashes
Date: 1995/12/19
Message-ID: <4b7g3k$92h@zk2nws.zko.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 122382577
distribution: world
references: <4b2q2j$i1l@harbinger.cc.monash.edu.au>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: denham@zk3.dec.com (Jeffrey M. Denham)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In article <4b2q2j$i1l@harbinger.cc.monash.edu.au>, jvf@aurora.cc.monash.edu.au (J Frankland) writes:
>The threads programming example in the Guide to DECthreads (accessible
>by the bookreader) which conducts a prime number search, crashes.
>It is Example 5-1.  After compiling with the -lpthreads link flag, and
>running it, it Seg Faults before it get to the main() function.

You're building the programs incorrectly. The flag is -threads, not -lpthreads
etc.

cc -o prog proc.c -lwhatever1 -lwhatever2 -threads

The -threads switch insures the proper ordering of the other libraries
specified with the libraries required for running threads programs.
Build with just -lpthreads leaves out a few vital libraries. The
correct hand-crafted link would look like this:

cc -o prog prog.c -lwhatever1 -lwhatever2 -lpthreads -lmach -lc_r

libmach is the library used by the threads library to Mach threads stuff
with the kernel. libc_r is the thread-safe reentrant C runtime library.

NOTE: with V4.0 of Digital UNIX, due early next year, building threads
programs will change slightly.

To build "legacy" threaded application (ones based on 1003.1c/D4, as
in V3.2C and below):

cc -o prog prog.c -threads

To build "new" threaded applications based on the final pthreads standard
(1003.1c/D10):

cc -o prog prog.c -pthread

Note also that libc_r will go away. Instead, the only and only libc is
now thread safe.

Hope this is off some help to you.
-- 

