From: lucia@savvy.zko.dec.com (Tim Lucia)
Subject: Re: Mysterious DEC OSF C++ compiler warning
Date: 1995/12/20
Message-ID: <4b99kg$ktg@zk2nws.zko.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 122367345
distribution: world
references: <4b4rog$m80@holly.cc.uleth.ca>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: lucia@zko.dec.com
newsgroups: comp.lang.c++,comp.unix.osf.osf1


#include <iostream.h>

class junk
        { double a;
          public:
          junk(void) {};
          junk(double x) { a = x;};
          junk & operator = (junk & x) { a = x.a; return(*this);};
        };

main()
{
    junk x;

    x = junk(2);
}

// ARM, 12.8 (pp. 295-296)
//
// If all bases and members of a class X have copy constructors accepting
// const arguments, the generated copy constructor for X will take a
// single argument of type const X&, as follows (double has the const ones)
//
// X::X(const X&)
//
// Otherwise it will take a single arugment of type X&:
//
// X::X(X&)
//
// Similarly, for assignment operators:
//
// If all bases...
//
// X& X::operator=(const X&)
//
// or
//
// X& X::operator=(X&) // which is what you have
//
// Since you declared a non-const assignment operator, then the constructor
// junk(2) does not match any declared constructor (default X& X::operator=(const X&))
// so you get the warning on conversion to using the non-const one.
//
// use this:   junk & operator = (const junk & x) { a = x.a; return(*this);};


Regards,
Tim

