references: <DJuz68.32E@world.std.com> 
organization: Irvine Compiler Corp., Irvine, California, USA
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.protocols.tcp-ip


I don't know the answer to your specific question, but I've found that
our Alpha/OSF1 (a "Jensen"--a 2000/300 EISA-based low-end alpha) is
unusually sensitive to ethernet hardware problems.

I just spent several hours trying to figure out why our alpha was
having trouble talking to one of our NCD X terminals, and it turned
out to be a bad segment of ethernet coax cable.  However, other
systems on our LAN--Sun SPARCstations, Pentium boxes running Linux,
old Integrated Solutions, VAX/VMS, and HP 700 systems--had no problems
popping up clients on the X terminal.  Only the alpha had problems.

To diagnose the problem, I just used "/usr/bin/ping" and watched the
packet sequence numbers.  It turned out that sequence numbers were
being skipped every so often (1, 2, 5, ...--hey, where're 3 and 4?
...that kind of thing), but only from the alpha.  It was pretty weird.

So, I replaced every single ethernet cable on the part of the coax
sub-loop that included the X terminal, and the problem went away.

Hope this helps...



