From: metsky@telemark.zk3.dec.com (David Metsky)
Subject: Re: Enforcing X Open Compliance in Digital Unix
Date: 1995/12/15
Message-ID: <4asqnh$s9r@lead.zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 121945746
distribution: world
references: <4amtbs$1u1@louie.udel.edu>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: metsky@zk3.dec.com (David Metsky)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


c89 -D_XOPEN_SOURCE -warnprotos

Use of the "c89" command will give you an environment that is a superset of the 
latest X/Open standard (XPG4 in V3.2C).  To get a strict X/Open environment the 
standard tells you to define the preprocessor symbol _XOPEN_SOURCE.  This will 
cause the system include files mentioned in XPG4 to limit their definitions to 
those specified in the standard.  In addition to this the "-warnprotos" option 
can be used to identify use of unprototyped functions at compile time.  All XPG4 
functions will be prototyped in the include file mentioned specified in XPG4.  
Use of include files not mentioned in XPG4 will reduce the effectiveness of this 
name-space restriction.

Other XPG4 branded systems will also have to follow the name-space rules 
activated with the -D_XOPEN_SOURCE define, but they may or may not provide an 
equivalent to the -warnprotos compiler option.

 -dave-
