> suppose i want to make restore over the existing files what would happen?.
> i'm thinking about using restore this way.
> 1) login as single user mode
> 2) mount /usr
> 3) cd /usr
> example to restore the usr partion /usr 
>  restore -rs 2   is this correct?

Try your restore like this

  restore -s2 -Yr

To restore the root partition...

	1. Boot your installation cdrom
	2. Select the system management option on the menu
	3. cd /dev
	4. MAKEDEV rzX tzX   (X being your appropriate drive numbers)
	5. newfs the root partition
	6. mount the partition on /mnt
	7. cd /mnt
	8. restore -Yr
	9. when the restore is finished, reboot your newly restored 
		root partition and restore the other two partitions.

