From: mdeters@cs.bgsu.edu (Morgan Deters)
Subject: Re: Mysterious DEC OSF C++ compiler warning
Date: 1995/12/18
Message-ID: <mdeters-1812951946010001@ppp-k-29.bgsu.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 122198853
references: <4b4rog$m80@holly.cc.uleth.ca>
organization: Bowling Green State University, Bowling Green, Ohio
newsgroups: comp.lang.c++,comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <4b4rog$m80@holly.cc.uleth.ca>, Marc Roussel
<roussel@henri.chem.uleth.ca> wrote:

> Here's a simple program for which the DEC OSF C++ compiler generates a (to me)
> mysterious warning:
> 
> // foo.h:
> class junk
>         { double a;
>           public:
>           junk(void) {};
>           junk(double x) { a = x;};
>           junk & operator = (junk & x) { a = x.a; return(*this);};
>         };
> 
> // foo.c
> #include <iostream.h>
> #include <math.h>
> #include "foo.h"
> 
> 
> main()
> {
>     junk x;
> 
>     x = junk(2);
> }
> 
> The warning is
> 
> foo.c:10: warning: In this statement, the initialization of a non-const
> reference requires a temporary for "junk(2)".

junk::operator=(junk&) takes a non-const reference as a parameter.  You
are supplying a temporary, junk(2), to the operator=() method.

If operator=() is not to modify its argument (which it really shouldn't
do, if you model your overloaded operators after built-in operator
concepts), declare operator=() as:
   junk& operator=(const junk& x) { a = x.a; return *this; }

This will clear the problem.

The compiler doesn't like it because the temporary junk(2) could be altered,
which is ridiculous because you no longer have access to it.
