I found for the 9gig disks that there is an "RZ" name for the 9gig Seagate
drives.  This is probably also true for the 4gig drives.  I found that
using scu to get the capacity of the drive, I got exactly the number of blocks
that an "rz59" has in /etc/disktab.

You might want to check the RPM.  The hawks are only 5400, the barracudas are
7200.  I think RPM goes somewhere on the disk label.

A lot of disklabel parameters don't matter anymore, I've been lead to
believe.

Attached is an excerpt from my notes.  Before leaving, tell me, do you think
one or two of those particular drives would fit properly in a DEC 3000 box
and not overheat?

	
Stephen Paul Carrier		carrier@nature.berkeley.edu
UNIX Systems Analyst		(510)643-7861
College of Natural Resources
University of California at Berkeley

-----
the following chickenscratch (c) Stephen Paul Carrier 1995

* Labelling an unlabelled disk.

Use "show dev" at the ">>" console to get the scsi address and bus of
the device.  Use the command "disklabel -re /dev/rrz10c rz59" for
example.  The device file should be the disk as a raw device, and the
type "rz59" is the standard 9gig size.  (see /etc/disktab for other
possibilities.)  To get the size of the disk, use the following
sequence:

# /sbin/scu -f /dev/rrz10c
scu> show capacity



