Hobbs) writes: 
<snip>
|> Therefore, if you use cc with -ieee_with_no_inexact then sqrt(-1.0) will
return 
|> a quietNaN and arithmetic on quietNaNs will *not* generate a signal
(unless you 
|> enable signaling--see the ieee(3) man page).  For historical reasons,
sqrt(-1.0) 
|> produces 0.0 when -ieee_with_no_inexact is not enabled.

According to ANSI C, unless you do some implementation defined magic (like
`enabling signals' etc.), sqrt(-1.0) must _not_ produce an exception. It can
return any value defined by the implementation.

