wp01@pirates.cs.swt.edu (Email Forwarding Account for Wuxu Peng) wrote:
>Our department is in the process of purchasing a Digital Alpha server.
>The primary choice at this moment is between 2100 4/275 and 2100 5/250.
>The latter is based on a new architecture and is claimed to have much
>better performance, although it's has a 250MHz CPU.  But the primary
>difference that I found so far is that 5/250 has a 4-MB third-level
>(off chip) cache, while 4/275 has a 4-MB (on board) cache.
>
>Price wise, the 5/250 model is about $8,000 more than 4/275 on the base
>configuration.  My question is that is this additional $8,000 justified?
>Our server will be targeted as a file/database server.
>
>Thanks.
>
>-- 
>
>--
>--wuxu peng (wp01@swt.edu)
>

I think this understates the difference.  We went from a 3000 Model
800 (4/200 equivalent) to a 2100 5/275 and saw an overall performance
improvement that was well beyond what you would project from the
clock rate.

My understanding is that the 5-series chips have more, and more
levels of, on-chip cache, and that they can perform two integer and
two floating point instructions at once, rather than one of each like
the 4-series chips. 

We are very happy with our 2100 5/275.

