From: skk@pa.dec.com (Stuart Kreitman)
Subject: Re: how to set kmem_debug on DEC
Date: 1995/12/12
Message-ID: <4aijhe$en9@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 121589968
references: <DIvy76.5I6@ampex.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

add the line:

	options KDEBUG

to /sys/conf/<MACHINE>, and rerun config. This is also a menu choice
during doconfig, might be easier to perform.

Once you have a kdebug'able kernel, you need to connect the host
and target machine serial ports with a null modem between them.
On the target machine:
	$ halt
	>>> set BOOT_OSFLAGS k
	>>> boot
On the host machine:
	$ dbx -remote

The kdebug kernel comes up a little way and sends a beacon down the wire.
If things aren't good, and the host dbx doesn't respond, then the target
won't come up.

It's really cool to copy the target kernel and driver source to the host,
then, you can do source level debugging on your driver. Yay!

So what kinda driver you got?

Stuart Kreitman
Faster Raster Systems

