newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

"Phil S. Wilshire" <Phil.wilshire@on-ramp.ior.com> writes:
>Check your compile options.
>I have found a 2:1 difference in the various flavors.

I think the OS can matter a good bit too, in terms of how efficient
library functions are.  I have access to a pair of identical machines,
one running DU3.2 and one running a beta version of DU4.0.  The
transcendental functions in the math library are markedly faster in
4.0, perhaps 25% or so for sin(), cos(), exp(), and pow() in the test
loops I ran.  The timings did depend a little bit on how the program
was compiled, but the overriding factor is the library routines
themselves--with dynamically linked executables, the timings were
correlated mainly to which machine I ran them on, while with
statically linked executables, the timings were strongly correlated to
which machine I compiled them on.

On the other hand, the 4.0 trigonometric routines are significantly
slower than the 3.2 routines when called with large (much bigger than
2*pi) arguments.

-- 
Brian R. Hunt
bhunt@ipst.umd.edu



