This info seems to have gotten back to DEC. When I called asking for 
Advfs info, there only response was "don't use it for spool", which I already
knew from the horror stories I'd read here.

What really bothers me is that with this "don't use it for news spool", DEC
seems to have neatly stepped back from a position that advfs is a general 
purpose filesystem alternative to UFS.

I say "it is a general purpose alternative to UFS" because there is a total
absense of info to the contrary in the 'advfs documentation' - quote marks
intentional.

When I called them up looking for some detailed technical info on how
stripe file allocation worked, I was told "our hints just cover not using it 
for news spool", and "it's based on the megatech file system, you might be
able to find some technical info on that system in a bookstore" - great.

The lack of even basic technical info is really amazing for a released product.

I found my first loosly related advfs bug this week, 3.2c only. A vdump of /, 
will somehow try to dump /proc (procfs), causing a bunch of 'invalid argument' 
messages to be displayed and vdump to return an error exit code. 
I was amazed, as it must have taken a lot of effort to make vdump look inside 
a totally different (and virtual) filesystem.

Apparantly there will be no patch (fix in 4.0), solution, umount /proc before 
running a vdump of /.

Apart from this, I've been fairly happy with advfs performance/functionality,
though I havn't tried clone filesets, trashcan directories, news spool etc.
We do our own OSF/1 custom installs, and root/usr works well
Just wish it had some decent documentation.

tony



