> Doesn't like restoring to OS/2 boxes when the backup is near the
> end of a tape, but that could be our beat-up drive.  That's the
> only problem we've had.  Oh, yes, one more problem., you can't
> hot-swap one machine for another during backup, that is, you can't
> restore a backup of 111.222.333.444 (cough) to 111.222.333.555
> (heh) if both boxes are OS/2, but you can if they're Sparcs.
> 

We've been using Legato for several years, backing up mostly UNIX
boxes (mostly sun).  I like the product, and would recommend it
highly.  I currently backup about 64 nodes, including desktops with
only 500 Mbytes to backup up to servers with dozens of Gbytes.

There is one problem that they (legato) and I have gone back and forth
on.  They say it's designed "that way" (ie, a feature) - I say, a bug.

When you have a group of nodes to be backed up, and the savegroup
begins, say, at 10:00pm on Thursday, the type of backup performed
(incremental or full) is determined by the date that device begins
backing up, not when the group started.  The result is that if
partition /X starts at 11:59, it'll get Thursday's schedule, but at
12:01, Friday's.  You can never guarantee which schedule you'll get, if
you cross midnight in the savegroup.

As a result, I start all my backups at 12:05am, thus losing 5 or so hours
of good backup time...  I don't understand why they can't use the start
of the group as the determining factor for all the individual backups
in the group, rather then the start of each individual save.  

Hope this help.

-cpf
