In <KHD.95Dec6100559@nano.atlas.de> khd@nano.atlas.de (Karl-Heinz Dewenter) writes:

>We have a problem with Digital UNIX, concerning the paging mechanism.
>Our process is extremely large, using about 480 Mbyte virtual memory.
>There is 256 Mbyte physical memory present.
>In some phases the program touches nearly every page sequentially.
>In this situation the system "pauses" for about 60-80 seconds.
...
>Does anybody know about this phenomenon?

Yes, it's usually called "thrashing" and it isn't limited to Digital UNIX.

>Is there any method to avoid this long time of inactivity?

Here are a couple of methods:

1. Redesign and reimplement your application, to avoid touching nearly
   every page sequentially.

2. Add another 256 MB of RAM.

You can also try running your process at lower priority.  I heard people
saying that this helps in certain cases.

Dan
