In Article <49lpig$dgk@masala.cc.uh.edu>
phys6k@menudo.uh.edu (Yan-Song Chen) writes:
>a stupid question:
>	What's difference between OSF/1 and DEC Unix?

"OSF/1" is what Digital used to call their Unix operating system for the Alpha
based processors. "Digital UNIX" is what they started calling it when they
recieved license to use the sacred name "UNIX" from X/Open. "DEC Unix" is
unknown to me. :-)

If it's V3.2 or higher, Digital calls it Digital UNIX and if below, "OSF/1".
But since it calls itself "OSF/1" and, I'm told, always will, I don't see any
reason not to continue to call it "OSF/1", but Digital will not.

If you are still confused, call a good tradmark/patent attorney. And note that
under trademark law and in UNIX, case is very significant.

