Yes, there is a work-around:

On Wed, 29 Nov 1995, chefren wrote:

> Gernot M. Fuchs wrote:
> > 
> > Hello,
> > 
> > I'm experiencing problems with colour mapping on my DEC Alphas running
> > OSF1 3.2 and Digital Unix respectively. When running Netscape on these
> > machines, it seems there are too view colours available to display the
> > graphics provided by the servers without dithering. Any other machine
> > here (SGI, Linux-PC and SUN) does this properly with Netscape.
> > 
> > My question is: Am I doing something wrong with my X-Server, or is this
> > a general limitation of the DEC X-Servers, or is this a Netscape
> > Problem?
> > 
> 
> IF SOMEONE KNOWS A SOLUTION FOR THIS PROBLEM: PLEASE HELP, I'M TRYING FOR 
> MONTHS!!!
> 

No it's not really a Netscape problem, except that Netscape tries to add
lots to the default color map. And I may not be using the exact correct
terms, but have encountered in several applications in the past the
limited number of 'active' colors available in the Digital UNIX color
map. (Makes for some very interesting pictures with color substitution.)
But buried in the Netscape help is the -install command line option, which
causes Netscape to use a private colormap, and get all its colors right.
Has the side effect that it makes all the background colors of the
inactive windows into unusual color combinations, but that's kind of
interesting too. One caveat, see further below.

From the Netscape help file:

Command line options 
     See the online release notes for more information. 

   -help  (lists all options)
   -geometry =WxH+X+Y (to position and size the window)
   -visual  (to use a specific server visual)
   -install (to install a private colormap)		<-- here's the one!
   -no-install (to use the default colormap)		
   -ncols  (when not using -install, set the maximum number of 
     colors to allocate for images)
   -mono (force 1-bit-deep image display)
   -xrm  (to set a specific X resource)
   -remote  (to execute a command in an 
     already-running Netscape process)
   -id  (the id of an X window to send the -remote 
     commands; if unspecified, the first window found is used)

Here's the caveat, from the release notes:

Applets will not display correctly on 8-bit visuals if you 
use the -install flag. 


