In Article <1995Dec1.094615.8268@pollux>
DavidW@dslak.co.nz (David Watterson) writes:
>
>I have recently sold a 2100 4/233 with Dec Unix and the C development 
>license.  The customer now wants to know what the C dev license is useful 
>for.  Digital suggested we include the license, so we did.  But is it 
>"required"?   The customer will do no development, and requires only the C 
>capabilities included in the OS.  Will he need the development license?

If they will need any C capabilities, the development license is appropriate.
The included C compiler is there to compile kernel code only. dbx will only run
on kernel code, so they will no be able to do ANY debug if they have to build
anything in C. And, finally, it's possible that some day Digital will ship with
NO C compiler. Sun already does this and I think SGI does, too. That would
leave them unable to compile at all on the system.

Of course, you could give them gcc and gdb as long as you could live with the
terms of the GNU GPL and have space for the sources of gcc and gdb.

