In article <49n6ul$1mt@netope.harvard.edu>, Marty Sasaki (sasaki@netop3.harvard.edu) wrote:

> So, my question is, is it safe to use the Advanced File System? It's
> features make it seem very desireable.

Indeed. That is the dilemma.

I went through AdvFS hell last spring. 1-3 crashes per day, all AdvFS
related. Being able to successfully backup an AdvFS domain was a 50/50
proposition. Through it all, however, I never lost a file system, 
except to hardware failure. 

Whether it's safe to use is very much a subjective judgement call.
There are folks who swear by it and have never had a moments troubles.
Others, like me, swear _at_ it, having seen it at its worst.

Here's my input, for what it's worth:

1)When it works, it's great. When it fails, look out, because the
depth of support and tools to bail you out just aren't there yet.

2) Beware flaky hardware. AdvFs is notoriously sensitive to bad
disks.

3) Beware missing features. Do you use quotas? Do you expect to be
able to restore a file system and still use quotas? You won't, unless
you've written a cron job to save quota information and a script
to rebuild the quota file. AdvFS vdumps don't dump quota information.

4) Plan to use it for a news spool? You've then entered the twilight
zone, where few have been able to return with solid answers about
what's going on. 

Despite all the problems I've had with AdvFS, I've not brought any
of my systems (news spool included) back to UFS. The features and
flexibility are too important to me. And it is, for the moment,
for me, for my environment, stable.

	- Saul
